**Cost**
*   Agentic search costs increase with the number of model steps taken during file searches and reads via LLM tokens (`agentic-search.md:2`).
*   RAG incurs initial setup costs for embeddings but has predictable retrieval costs based on similarity search and inference usage (`rag-overview.md:1-2`).

**Freshness**
*   Agentic search offers dynamic freshness because agents "search and read files as needed" without requiring an index, ensuring results are always fresh (`agentic-search.md:2`).
*   RAG suffers from potential staleness because its indexing requirement means data can become outdated before retrieval (`agentic-search.md:2`).
*   *Note on general freshness mechanisms:* Internal docs describe API polling leading to stale data via `max_staleness` thresholds (https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/building-microservices-with-java/9781491961855/ch02.html), WebSockets providing live streams despite overhead (https://microservices.io/patterns/data/pub-sub/), and Server-Sent Events (SSE) offering near-real-time data with latency trade-offs (https://martinfowler.com/articles/rabbitmq-in-production.html).

**Accuracy on Exact Error Codes**
*   Agentic search performs well on exact error code lookup, as "exact matches (error codes, IDs) work well with regex search" (`agentic-search.md:2`).
*   There is a specific file `error-codes.md` dedicated to searching for error codes, highlighting this as a key evaluation area (`error-codes.md:1`).

**Disagreements/Conflicts**
*   The findings contain no direct disagreement between Agentic Search and RAG; rather, they present a trade-off where Agentic Search favors freshness and exact-match capability without an index, while RAG favors predictability and lower ongoing compute costs per query but risks indexing staleness.
